    "text": "{\n  \"anomaly_detected\": true,\n  \"confidence\": 0.97,\n  \"anomalies\": [\n    {\n      \"category\": \"bridged_nets\",\n      \"components\": [\"SW6\", \"R34\", \"R35\"],\n      \"pins\": [\"SW6.1\", \"SW6.2\", \"SW6.3\", \"SW6.4\", \"R34.1\", \"R34.2\", \"R35.1\", \"R35.2\"],\n      \"nets\": [\"/CAN0_N\", \"/CAN0_P\", \"/CAN1_N\", \"/CAN1_P\", \"Net-(R34-Pad2)\", \"Net-(R35-Pad2)\"],\n      \"observed\": \"The two poles of SW6 connect pins 1\u20132 and 3\u20134. Pin 1 is connected through R34 (120R) to /CAN0_N, pin 2 through R35 (120R) to /CAN1_N, pin 3 is on /CAN1_P, and pin 4 is on /CAN0_P. Closing the respective DIP switches therefore couples the two CAN negative nets through R34 and R35 in series, and directly shorts the two CAN positive nets.\",\n      \"expected\": \"CAN0 and CAN1 should remain independent isolated buses, as specified. If SW6 is intended to enable termination, each termination resistor should connect across the positive and negative lines of its own bus rather than couple the two buses.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"source\": \"netlist\",\n          \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"SW6\\\") (pin \\\"4\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\")) on /CAN0_P; (node (ref \\\"SW6\\\") (pin \\\"3\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\")) on /CAN1_P\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"source\": \"netlist\",\n          \"quote\": \"/CAN0_N has J2.8, J13.8, U11.6, and R34.1; /CAN1_N has J2.3, J13.3, U10.6, and R35.1\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"source\": \"netlist\",\n          \"quote\": \"Net-(R34-Pad2) contains R34.2 and SW6.1; Net-(R35-Pad2) contains R35.2 and SW6.2\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"source\": \"spec\",\n          \"quote\": \"two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller through an isolated CAN transceiver\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"When the relevant DIP switches are closed, the two CAN buses are no longer independent: their positive lines are shorted together and their negative lines are coupled through 240 ohms. This can interfere with bus signaling and undermines the intended isolation between CAN0 and CAN1.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.97\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"SW6\u2019s net connections allow it to couple the two otherwise isolated CAN buses: one switch pole directly joins their positive lines, while the other connects their negative lines through two 120-ohm resistors. This conflicts with the specified independent CAN0 and CAN1 interfaces.\"\n}",
